-- catalog_sqlite_schema_v1_checks.sql — reproductions for codex's findings against schema v1.2:
-- the 12 of the first pass (docs/reviews/codex/
-- codex-review-contract-freeze-sqlite-schema-v1-step-b-2026-10-17.md, blocks F1…F12, D530) and the 6
-- of the r2 closing pass (codex-review-contract-freeze-r2-closing-pass-2026-10-17.md, blocks F5r3,
-- F8r3, F9r3, N1, N2, N3). r3 changed the r2 blocks only where v1.2 renamed or moved what they
-- touch (F1/fixture: N1 snapshot columns; F5: resolvedByRule; F6: N1 audit columns; F9: imported
-- caption in derived_unverified, no personSearched); every r2 assertion is kept.
-- Run from the repository root:   sqlite3 :memory: < docs/design/catalog_sqlite_schema_v1_checks.sql
-- Each block reproduces the finding's scenario and ends in `SELECT 'F<N> ok' WHERE …`, which prints
-- only when every assertion of that block holds (assertions are rows in t_assert). The schema load
-- and the fixture run under `.bail on`: a schema that does not load stops the script. The finding
-- blocks then run under `.bail off`, because each block contains statements the schema must
-- REFUSE, and the sqlite3 shell counts errors cumulatively — re-enabling `.bail on` after one
-- expected refusal exits at the next statement (verified on 3.45.1). Every expected refusal is
-- announced by a "(expected refusal: …)" line and followed by an assertion that the refused change
-- did not land; any error NOT preceded by such a line is a failure. The last line,
-- `all checks ok (85 assertions)`, prints only when every assertion in the file holds. The shell's
-- exit status is 1 because of the expected refusals; the printed lines are the verdict.
-- Last run: sqlite3 3.45.1 (Linux), 2026-10-17 — F1…F12, D530, F5r3, F8r3, F9r3, N1, N2, N3 ok,
-- integrity_check ok, all checks ok (76 assertions); 56 refusals, each announced, none unannounced.
-- Rerun 2026-10-17 against schema v1.4 (C′ part 2a): sqlite3 3.54.0 (macOS 27.0.1) — the same
-- lines plus F5final ok; 57 refusals (the F5final one now announced). VideoScanCore's
-- CatalogDataSchemaTests runs this file block by block against the GRDB migration's database
-- (the two seeding INSERTs below are replaced by the migration's own rows, see the test).
-- Rerun 2026-10-17 against schema v1.5 (codex C′ pass on the built store, blocks C1 and C7 added:
-- docs/reviews/codex/codex-review-sqlite-store-parts-1-2a-step-c-2026-10-17.md): sqlite3 3.54.0
-- (macOS 27.0.1) — F1…N3, C1, C7 ok, integrity_check ok, all checks ok (85 assertions); 63
-- refusals, each announced, none unannounced; F5final ok.
-- Generated by a throwaway script; digests are 'a'/'b'/'c' x 64, row ids 00000000-…-<n>.
.bail on
.headers off
.mode list
.read docs/design/catalog_sqlite_schema_v1.sql

CREATE TEMP TABLE t_assert (label TEXT NOT NULL, ok INTEGER);

